00:07:49.645 --> 00:07:54.005
Matheus because I love learning more about science.

00:07:54.005 --> 00:07:57.080
I'm not a quantum scientists on the panel,

00:07:57.080 --> 00:08:02.415
but I'm interested in hearing about the paths of Zlatko and Teresa.

00:08:02.415 --> 00:08:04.665
Thank you for having me Florian.

00:08:04.665 --> 00:08:08.115
Thank you. That's my pleasure.

00:08:08.115 --> 00:08:12.314
Let's jump right in on the questions.

00:08:12.314 --> 00:08:15.290
I think that's going to be the first question I have is,

00:08:15.290 --> 00:08:21.350
what it's like to work in industry because people asking that question,

00:08:21.350 --> 00:08:23.570
I've been at school for all this time.

00:08:23.570 --> 00:08:27.095
You go to high school and college, grad school maybe.

00:08:27.095 --> 00:08:29.350
What is it like? What's the difference?

00:08:29.350 --> 00:08:32.640
Because all of you were in labs like students and so.

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:34.530
Who wants to start with this question,

00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:36.165
what's the main difference?

00:08:36.165 --> 00:08:44.170
What you should look for and all of that? Who wants to jump first?

00:08:44.540 --> 00:08:50.030
I can maybe lead it off if you like then Teresa you can jump in.

00:08:50.030 --> 00:08:54.575
I think maybe the first thing to just acknowledge is that it's not monolithic,

00:08:54.575 --> 00:08:57.110
whether it's an academia or in industry.

00:08:57.110 --> 00:09:01.160
I was thinking about this a little bit because the experience I

00:09:01.160 --> 00:09:05.920
think I had starting at Yale in my graduate degree in 2010,

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:09.170
2011 is very different in a sense from experience as

00:09:09.170 --> 00:09:13.445
students starting today would have the whole landscape of quantum has changed a lot.

00:09:13.445 --> 00:09:17.315
I think maybe that's the caveat to begin with,

00:09:17.315 --> 00:09:21.140
is that it's easy to plug the blanket statement of industry,

00:09:21.140 --> 00:09:25.579
but really there's so many different flavors and that's part of what your experiences.

00:09:25.579 --> 00:09:28.460
It feels like a bigger world in some sense.

00:09:28.460 --> 00:09:30.595
It's a lot more dynamic.

00:09:30.595 --> 00:09:33.770
Part of what you'll definitely see in a lot of industry,

00:09:33.770 --> 00:09:36.655
especially as you move into more technology industry.

00:09:36.655 --> 00:09:40.760
The industry that quantum is headed into is that it's a little closer

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:43.130
to the machine learning tech industry where

00:09:43.130 --> 00:09:46.115
things happen in two-year cycles, three years cycles.

00:09:46.115 --> 00:09:47.750
The pace is definitely

00:09:47.750 --> 00:09:53.430
one major change that you have in terms of certain schedules and pace.

00:09:54.050 --> 00:09:58.670
Also you will work in much larger groups and teams.

00:09:58.670 --> 00:10:02.770
Typically, it's definitely more oriented.

00:10:02.770 --> 00:10:04.950
They are potentially creating stuff.

00:10:04.950 --> 00:10:09.840
It's a little less significance driven than published this paper or so forth.

00:10:10.060 --> 00:10:12.730
I think at the same time though,

00:10:12.730 --> 00:10:15.500
really depends because there are many different cultures.

00:10:15.500 --> 00:10:16.630
You had a startup panel,

00:10:16.630 --> 00:10:20.930
it's going to have a different slant on it versus a large organization like IBM,

00:10:20.930 --> 00:10:24.535
which is huge and it's very global and worldwide.

00:10:24.535 --> 00:10:30.150
You're going to work with people that come from tons of different backgrounds.

00:10:30.150 --> 00:10:32.300
This is one of the maybe biggest differences.

00:10:32.300 --> 00:10:34.445
Now, I'm not constantly surrounded by

00:10:34.445 --> 00:10:38.155
like-minded people who had the exact same training for the last 20 years as me.

00:10:38.155 --> 00:10:41.510
In physics, I'm working with software developers,

00:10:41.510 --> 00:10:43.430
product designers, physicists, engineers,

00:10:43.430 --> 00:10:45.730
material scientists who have different trainings.

00:10:45.730 --> 00:10:49.545
Whose value system is a bit different than mine.

00:10:49.545 --> 00:10:52.060
We're not all after the same thing.

00:10:52.060 --> 00:10:56.800
I think readjusting my own values in this environment,

00:10:56.800 --> 00:11:01.065
it is one thing that I think inadvertently will happen.

00:11:01.065 --> 00:11:02.560
That's also true and active too.

00:11:02.560 --> 00:11:03.845
I think as you go to next stage,

00:11:03.845 --> 00:11:05.540
those will have to update.

00:11:05.540 --> 00:11:09.110
But it's certainly a lot of interaction,

00:11:09.110 --> 00:11:12.480
a lot more team focused in a sense.

00:11:13.550 --> 00:11:16.860
Maybe I'll pass the ball up to Teresa because I could

00:11:16.860 --> 00:11:20.400
probably keep going here for another 10 minutes about this.

00:11:24.620 --> 00:11:26.700
Riffing off the first thing,

00:11:26.700 --> 00:11:34.280
you said about it not being monolithic industry versus academia.

00:11:34.280 --> 00:11:37.970
Part of that is that there are many partnerships and programs that

00:11:37.970 --> 00:11:42.839
foster collaboration between academic groups and industry.

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:50.365
That can take many forms of either sharing technology or doing like a postdoc,

00:11:50.365 --> 00:11:58.135
visiting at research lab in the industry part.

00:11:58.135 --> 00:12:00.925
There's a lot of activity going on there,

00:12:00.925 --> 00:12:09.170
and a lot of collaborations are now being encouraged by the government and new programs.

00:12:09.240 --> 00:12:13.765
One thing that I really appreciate about working in industry

00:12:13.765 --> 00:12:18.385
over academic labs that I have been in is that,

00:12:18.385 --> 00:12:20.230
you get to participate in

00:12:20.230 --> 00:12:24.970
really efficient technological developments in a larger team where there's

00:12:24.970 --> 00:12:27.760
career experts in their specialized roles that can

00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:32.815
be really relied upon to perform their function at the highest level.

00:12:32.815 --> 00:12:43.075
Like we have a hardware team that has been doing hardware development for decades,

00:12:43.075 --> 00:12:48.040
and gone through many technological innovations over that time,

00:12:48.040 --> 00:12:50.620
and they know how to like carry things forward.

00:12:50.620 --> 00:12:55.750
We have a fabrication team that's also very high in expertise.

00:12:55.750 --> 00:12:57.520
The software team.

00:12:57.520 --> 00:13:03.445
They're all really good, and it's not like that in academia where by design,

00:13:03.445 --> 00:13:09.535
there's a lot of education going on and there's a constant export of talent.

00:13:09.535 --> 00:13:15.115
That's where you go and learn everything, and there are many.

00:13:15.115 --> 00:13:19.520
That's where all the smartest people in the world I write is that the universities.

00:13:20.910 --> 00:13:25.180
But then once a person becomes an expert in something,

00:13:25.180 --> 00:13:28.285
they leave or go and learn something else.

00:13:28.285 --> 00:13:31.210
I guess what I appreciated about the industrial environment is

00:13:31.210 --> 00:13:35.200
that expert stay doing what their expert at.

00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:38.815
Then by constructing the teams efficiently,

00:13:38.815 --> 00:13:42.160
we're able to work together to build something that's much

00:13:42.160 --> 00:13:46.520
bigger than you can generally achieve in a research group.

00:13:47.820 --> 00:13:50.170
Now that's a really good point.

00:13:50.170 --> 00:13:55.930
I think we we've saw that in the different panels that we had over the last year.

00:13:55.930 --> 00:13:59.650
Talking about like if you're joining an academy group as a PI,

00:13:59.650 --> 00:14:01.870
or if you're turning in a startup, or industry,

00:14:01.870 --> 00:14:04.990
you have really different roles because the goal ought not the same.

00:14:04.990 --> 00:14:10.450
I think academia has more of an educational purpose to train people.

00:14:10.450 --> 00:14:13.390
Startups will try to disrupt and find new ways,

00:14:13.390 --> 00:14:15.430
and industry has more of a product and

00:14:15.430 --> 00:14:18.040
like particularly in mind to have some something and,

00:14:18.040 --> 00:14:20.260
so I think that's something that you want to consider also

00:14:20.260 --> 00:14:23.980
for your research in carriers like what's the angle?

00:14:23.980 --> 00:14:25.570
What would you like to do in your life?

00:14:25.570 --> 00:14:28.180
Do you want to teach students?

00:14:28.180 --> 00:14:29.620
Do you want to create serve?

00:14:29.620 --> 00:14:32.500
Do you want to create a product that could be used

00:14:32.500 --> 00:14:36.175
widely by the total population of by system?

00:14:36.175 --> 00:14:38.380
That's something to also keep in mind.

00:14:38.380 --> 00:14:40.945
Since you have so many options on that,

00:14:40.945 --> 00:14:43.960
can you talk a little bit about what's your

00:14:43.960 --> 00:14:47.710
day-to-day like is in industry for people who have no idea?

00:14:47.710 --> 00:14:50.770
Because I think the big idea

00:14:50.770 --> 00:14:53.620
comes from the fact that we have one word to decry all of that,

00:14:53.620 --> 00:14:55.195
but there's so many different jobs,

00:14:55.195 --> 00:14:57.250
and so what do you do?

00:14:57.250 --> 00:15:00.230
Like if you if you have a typical jobs?

00:15:00.930 --> 00:15:06.265
I can maybe jump in here and build off of what Teresa said,

00:15:06.265 --> 00:15:08.980
and I'll see,

00:15:08.980 --> 00:15:11.110
like teaching and all these different things like initially I

00:15:11.110 --> 00:15:14.650
think before joining the industry,

00:15:14.650 --> 00:15:18.370
research side of it, I thought there was less of that,

00:15:18.370 --> 00:15:21.625
but actually we have a giant team focused on education.

00:15:21.625 --> 00:15:24.610
One thing I never anticipated, is,

00:15:24.610 --> 00:15:26.920
I've taught it to summer schools now on like

00:15:26.920 --> 00:15:29.950
introduction to quantum hardware and introduction to quantum noise,

00:15:29.950 --> 00:15:32.620
personally had like 4,000 something students.

00:15:32.620 --> 00:15:36.415
Second one had like 5,000 plus students global, worldwide.

00:15:36.415 --> 00:15:40.165
I think one thing I've found is that there's

00:15:40.165 --> 00:15:46.420
just a pretty big world and usually if you really want to be happy,

00:15:46.420 --> 00:15:49.645
you have to align your interests with the larger interests.

00:15:49.645 --> 00:15:56.140
Oftentimes if you're doing a degree in something like quantum,

00:15:56.140 --> 00:15:58.120
you're usually pretty smart, resourceful person,

00:15:58.120 --> 00:16:00.880
so you can find those potentials.

00:16:00.880 --> 00:16:05.335
That's why a typical day for me at least has evolved a lot.

00:16:05.335 --> 00:16:08.770
Initially, I've had days where it was just very focused on work.

00:16:08.770 --> 00:16:14.140
Nowadays it's a lot of more mission building and team-building as well.

00:16:14.140 --> 00:16:17.470
Trying to scale as well.

00:16:17.470 --> 00:16:23.415
I think the interpersonal becomes a lot more of your time as well.

00:16:23.415 --> 00:16:25.905
I think typically in industry because you're really working in

00:16:25.905 --> 00:16:28.920
teams and take your mission to greater things.

00:16:28.920 --> 00:16:31.290
It becomes more of a mission tuba.

00:16:31.290 --> 00:16:36.060
I've had sprints where it's been just software development,

00:16:36.060 --> 00:16:39.495
and sprints where it's been a lot of reading papers or physics.

00:16:39.495 --> 00:16:43.285
At least in my case, it's been quite colorful,

00:16:43.285 --> 00:16:46.614
and it keeps evolving and shifting.

00:16:46.614 --> 00:16:49.600
It's been a lot more dynamic in that sense,

00:16:49.600 --> 00:16:52.500
so not sure if that's helpful,

00:16:52.500 --> 00:16:55.065
but hopefully it helps paint a picture.

00:16:55.065 --> 00:17:00.090
I mean, everything is very helpful because what we're trying to do is like trying to

00:17:00.090 --> 00:17:05.455
see like how people who have similar backgrounds experienced their new role in history?

00:17:05.455 --> 00:17:07.225
I think it's very helpful capability know

00:17:07.225 --> 00:17:09.940
exactly what it is because somebody who doesn't like to teach,

00:17:09.940 --> 00:17:12.640
end up being in your position might be disappointed that

00:17:12.640 --> 00:17:16.060
he was like they were finding something slightly different.

00:17:16.060 --> 00:17:20.830
It's very nice.

00:17:20.830 --> 00:17:22.160
Go on.

00:17:22.260 --> 00:17:24.925
Keep going, I will show you.

00:17:24.925 --> 00:17:29.710
I guess the thought I had here on one piece of advice,

00:17:29.710 --> 00:17:33.415
I think when I was interviewing and applying was,

00:17:33.415 --> 00:17:35.860
there's no perfect position for you.

00:17:35.860 --> 00:17:38.575
You ideally, you define your own position

00:17:38.575 --> 00:17:41.800
and that's the only time you're really going to have that position,

00:17:41.800 --> 00:17:45.700
and I've had the chance to keep reshaping,

00:17:45.700 --> 00:17:48.010
and so I think maybe that's one thing to

00:17:48.010 --> 00:17:50.650
keep in mind is like you have an agency in this,

00:17:50.650 --> 00:17:54.340
it's not like you have to fit in a fixed bucket and fit the mold.

00:17:54.340 --> 00:17:59.095
Like you have agency, you have flexibility. I'll leave it at that.

00:17:59.095 --> 00:18:02.440
That's something that we'll talk about in a few moments later.

00:18:02.440 --> 00:18:06.235
We're going to let Teresa talk about experience in a child,

00:18:06.235 --> 00:18:08.335
and then we'll talk about how you can craft

00:18:08.335 --> 00:18:11.750
your your job to really match what your interests are.

00:18:12.090 --> 00:18:16.180
I think that's a really excellent point that you're emphasizing

00:18:16.180 --> 00:18:19.570
Zlatko that you have agency to craft your own experience.

00:18:19.570 --> 00:18:25.705
That's especially true after you like done your service of a year,

00:18:25.705 --> 00:18:33.355
maybe at the company to like show that you're smart and can work with people.

00:18:33.355 --> 00:18:37.330
Then like you can move

00:18:37.330 --> 00:18:43.870
into what you figure out is your ideal role as you figure that out,

00:18:43.870 --> 00:18:48.519
or if you want to try out new things within the same company.

00:18:48.519 --> 00:18:51.790
Thankfully, like you said, Zlatko,

00:18:51.790 --> 00:18:57.190
I've been able to keep my experience dynamic and try out some different things,

00:18:57.190 --> 00:19:00.175
and day-to-day is not always the same.

00:19:00.175 --> 00:19:02.350
When I first started the company,

00:19:02.350 --> 00:19:05.905
I was mostly doing experimental measurements,

00:19:05.905 --> 00:19:08.845
measuring the quantum devices.

00:19:08.845 --> 00:19:13.240
I would go in and do electrical test on devices,

00:19:13.240 --> 00:19:15.970
sit down at a dilution refrigerator,

00:19:15.970 --> 00:19:18.234
or remote into a computer,

00:19:18.234 --> 00:19:21.715
whereas accessing a device that's cool down in a refrigerator,

00:19:21.715 --> 00:19:25.915
and do electrical test on it.

00:19:25.915 --> 00:19:29.530
One difference between my experience here

00:19:29.530 --> 00:19:32.965
and graduate school is that we have a lot more meetings,

00:19:32.965 --> 00:19:37.090
because we have this specialized teams,

00:19:37.090 --> 00:19:41.110
and spend more time getting together

00:19:41.110 --> 00:19:45.730
to directly provide feedback between the different teams.

00:19:45.730 --> 00:19:47.320
As part of the measurement team,

00:19:47.320 --> 00:19:52.990
I was reporting the results of measurements back to fabrication team,

00:19:52.990 --> 00:19:55.840
and also interfacing a lot with

00:19:55.840 --> 00:19:59.930
a theory and modeling teams that we could validate their models.

00:20:02.400 --> 00:20:06.490
That's been fun and as my experience has evolved,

00:20:06.490 --> 00:20:10.340
I am now more interested in software.

00:20:11.820 --> 00:20:16.390
I think that's the interesting thing about industry is that you can find,

00:20:16.390 --> 00:20:19.630
especially if you join big groups or big companies,

00:20:19.630 --> 00:20:23.920
you have different nodes and then you can pick and involve in

00:20:23.920 --> 00:20:28.840
the same company in a way that will not be necessarily possible in Academia,

00:20:28.840 --> 00:20:34.870
I think, because you cannot change your group goals like every given on then.

00:20:34.870 --> 00:20:38.650
I think now we can jump into more of the nitty-gritty of like,

00:20:38.650 --> 00:20:40.720
how did you land that job?

00:20:40.720 --> 00:20:43.435
How did you prepare for the job search?

00:20:43.435 --> 00:20:45.205
Why were you interested in?

00:20:45.205 --> 00:20:49.450
Then also, what are the advice that you can give to people who are seeking job in

00:20:49.450 --> 00:20:54.920
industry or how can they prepare to work on that?

00:20:55.740 --> 00:20:58.330
Anybody wants to start?

00:20:58.330 --> 00:21:02.200
Great. Oh, I can read that off if you want because

00:21:02.200 --> 00:21:06.590
I started on the other side and now I'm more on the hiring side.

00:21:07.320 --> 00:21:10.480
Yeah, I have two job postings I have actually.

00:21:10.480 --> 00:21:14.500
One is on casket metal more software developer physicist focus.

00:21:14.500 --> 00:21:16.915
The other one is really a more researcher level focus.

00:21:16.915 --> 00:21:18.325
Those are two different roles.

00:21:18.325 --> 00:21:20.740
For the different roles that you're applying for,

00:21:20.740 --> 00:21:23.215
you're going to have different processes.

00:21:23.215 --> 00:21:31.840
Exactly. I think the first thing

00:21:31.840 --> 00:21:33.790
you can always start with to prepare is just

00:21:33.790 --> 00:21:36.445
yourself in your own background and material usually.

00:21:36.445 --> 00:21:38.680
If you're in science or a researcher,

00:21:38.680 --> 00:21:42.685
you'll get asked to tell people about your research, your work.

00:21:42.685 --> 00:21:46.585
You want to make sure that that's fresh in your mind, you're competent about it.

00:21:46.585 --> 00:21:50.065
Oftentimes you'll give a talk or a presentation.

00:21:50.065 --> 00:21:52.645
I had to give a talk and a presentation.

00:21:52.645 --> 00:21:57.040
I had probably given that talk and presentation like 18,

00:21:57.040 --> 00:22:02.065
19 times before giving it in many other rounds of interviews.

00:22:02.065 --> 00:22:04.870
I went maybe extra. I don't think you need to do it 20 times,

00:22:04.870 --> 00:22:10.090
but you can really prepare that and have a sensitive questions ready.

00:22:10.090 --> 00:22:15.190
I think the other thing folks look for a lot isn't just a technical competence.

00:22:15.190 --> 00:22:18.775
That's like, let's get you in the door, but that's the bar.

00:22:18.775 --> 00:22:21.355
Is this a person that we can get along with?

00:22:21.355 --> 00:22:25.405
Is this a person that seems like they actually want this?

00:22:25.405 --> 00:22:28.975
Are they playing for the right reasons? Are they motivated?

00:22:28.975 --> 00:22:30.160
Are they eager about this?

00:22:30.160 --> 00:22:31.540
Because you can have the right skills,

00:22:31.540 --> 00:22:33.310
but if you don't have the affinity for the mission,

00:22:33.310 --> 00:22:35.110
you're going to end up leaving or you're going to not

00:22:35.110 --> 00:22:37.090
just enjoy the job and not do a great job.

00:22:37.090 --> 00:22:38.875
Affinity is another key thing,

00:22:38.875 --> 00:22:41.900
like demonstrating that in some way.

00:22:43.350 --> 00:22:53.770
Then as you get to more software or more of the different more desirable positions,

00:22:53.770 --> 00:22:57.145
especially like in the new climate,

00:22:57.145 --> 00:22:59.755
you're often going to have a coding interview,

00:22:59.755 --> 00:23:01.030
a behavioral interview,

00:23:01.030 --> 00:23:02.410
and a technical interview.

00:23:02.410 --> 00:23:04.990
Then if you pass those you talk to more team members,

00:23:04.990 --> 00:23:09.370
more for culture fit and how can we get along because we only need to play together.

00:23:09.370 --> 00:23:12.625
The coding interview, there's many books and coding interviews

00:23:12.625 --> 00:23:16.645
or if you've gone for a software position, those really matter.

00:23:16.645 --> 00:23:19.150
You want to know whatever language it is,

00:23:19.150 --> 00:23:22.030
Python you might, there's like thousands of

00:23:22.030 --> 00:23:25.150
resources online for coding interviews that will walk you through the basics.

00:23:25.150 --> 00:23:28.600
If you're a physicist, sometimes those coding interviews are more like okay,

00:23:28.600 --> 00:23:30.610
can you be proficient enough to do your job,

00:23:30.610 --> 00:23:33.175
but that's not weight is heavily.

00:23:33.175 --> 00:23:37.150
You might have a physicist interview where you get asked, what's an Ising model?

00:23:37.150 --> 00:23:38.410
What are spin interactions?

00:23:38.410 --> 00:23:43.315
How do you measure T1 or T2 depending on if you go into measurement team?

00:23:43.315 --> 00:23:47.260
Oftentimes you want to understand because the job postings can be quite big.

00:23:47.260 --> 00:23:49.525
You want to understand from the hiring manager of the head,

00:23:49.525 --> 00:23:53.275
like what's really the general focus of this role?

00:23:53.275 --> 00:23:57.250
Then sometimes you have behavioral interviews,

00:23:57.250 --> 00:23:58.885
so you can look those up.

00:23:58.885 --> 00:24:01.974
Depends on different companies do it differently.

00:24:01.974 --> 00:24:05.110
There's no place that has the exact same process.

00:24:05.110 --> 00:24:08.485
But really the idea there is just to make sure that,

00:24:08.485 --> 00:24:13.540
you have a good mindset right.

00:24:13.540 --> 00:24:18.620
Maybe I'll leave it off to Teresa to add on more here.

00:24:20.730 --> 00:24:24.410
Sure. I like what you said.

00:24:24.480 --> 00:24:27.145
I would add to that.

00:24:27.145 --> 00:24:32.455
Don't try to squeeze yourself into a box that you think

00:24:32.455 --> 00:24:38.020
the ideal candidate for a given position is describing.

00:24:38.020 --> 00:24:41.350
Oftentimes, the job requisitions are written

00:24:41.350 --> 00:24:44.935
in a way that I think you said opaque psycho.

00:24:44.935 --> 00:24:46.315
Adds are like that too.

00:24:46.315 --> 00:24:52.555
I'm not sure why they pass through some HR filter before they go out there.

00:24:52.555 --> 00:24:56.500
Anyway, like generally what places are more looking

00:24:56.500 --> 00:25:00.820
for is what kind of a scientist are you?

00:25:00.820 --> 00:25:03.025
Do you have the affinity

00:25:03.025 --> 00:25:09.550
for doing something that is matching with the mission of the organization?

00:25:09.550 --> 00:25:12.110
Are you good cultural fit?

00:25:13.860 --> 00:25:16.150
Yeah, I guess,

00:25:16.150 --> 00:25:21.055
don't try to squeeze yourself into a box because you don't know what that box looks like.

00:25:21.055 --> 00:25:25.070
The company wants to hire a good scientist.

00:25:25.590 --> 00:25:28.075
Once you're at the company,

00:25:28.075 --> 00:25:36.980
you generally will have some freedom to be you at your best.

00:25:38.970 --> 00:25:44.785
I guess one more thing about giving a research presentation.

00:25:44.785 --> 00:25:51.835
It's like, Zlatco describes when you go to do a presentation.

00:25:51.835 --> 00:26:00.280
It's just like doing interview talk for a postdoc or professorship candidacy.

00:26:00.280 --> 00:26:04.030
Usually you talk about some prior research project,

00:26:04.030 --> 00:26:08.120
come into basically recycle your thesis defense presentation.

00:26:08.220 --> 00:26:11.890
But my piece of advice is that you need

00:26:11.890 --> 00:26:16.615
to communicate something technical and prove that you could do that.

00:26:16.615 --> 00:26:21.625
But you also have the opportunity to share more about yourself.

00:26:21.625 --> 00:26:24.250
What kind of a scientist are you?

00:26:24.250 --> 00:26:30.115
What challenges have you encountered and had to overcome?

00:26:30.115 --> 00:26:34.090
How do you work with and appreciate other people?

00:26:34.090 --> 00:26:40.375
If you can add little anecdotes to that end in there.

00:26:40.375 --> 00:26:43.870
More than just conveying physics information.

00:26:43.870 --> 00:26:46.100
We really look for that.

00:26:46.380 --> 00:26:53.395
Yeah. I can maybe add to that also because people look for cultural phenols.

00:26:53.395 --> 00:26:56.140
What does that even mean? What are they actually talking about?

00:26:56.140 --> 00:27:00.130
I think a lot of times that's the thing where it's not like you have to exceed,

00:27:00.130 --> 00:27:03.040
its not like you need an A plus, you just need a pass.

00:27:03.040 --> 00:27:06.745
Is this somebody that I could see myself getting along with?

00:27:06.745 --> 00:27:08.920
It's the thing that's more making sure there's

00:27:08.920 --> 00:27:13.240
no red flags like this isn't just going to derail the mission.

00:27:13.240 --> 00:27:17.425
I think just don't over stress about that as well.

00:27:17.425 --> 00:27:21.130
Then I think like Teresa said,

00:27:21.130 --> 00:27:24.160
yeah make sure that you generate yourself.

00:27:24.160 --> 00:27:26.680
One thing that I do notice folks,

00:27:26.680 --> 00:27:29.185
sometimes an interview processes though

00:27:29.185 --> 00:27:32.230
what oftentimes will happen is they'll get stressed or freaked out,

00:27:32.230 --> 00:27:36.350
but they don't know the answer to something that we can't quite remember it.

00:27:36.570 --> 00:27:40.705
The point of these interviews isn't for you to get it right.

00:27:40.705 --> 00:27:42.444
Most of the companies,

00:27:42.444 --> 00:27:47.470
they are just testing for the boundaries of your knowledge and just getting a sense.

00:27:47.470 --> 00:27:50.949
Actually as the hiring managers or the interviewers

00:27:50.949 --> 00:27:56.020
job oftentimes is to just keep asking questions until you can't answer anymore.

00:27:56.020 --> 00:27:59.920
They just want to see where that boundary is and if part of it is shallow,

00:27:59.920 --> 00:28:02.200
that's okay because you've probably got another part

00:28:02.200 --> 00:28:04.885
that's deep and you can help steer the conversation too,

00:28:04.885 --> 00:28:08.365
it's not always clear to the hiring person exactly who they're dealing with.

00:28:08.365 --> 00:28:10.720
They just have three minutes right before this talk

00:28:10.720 --> 00:28:13.465
between the other talk to look at your CV.

00:28:13.465 --> 00:28:15.610
You can help guide them as well.

00:28:15.610 --> 00:28:17.110
These are things I'm strong at,

00:28:17.110 --> 00:28:18.985
these are things I love to do.

00:28:18.985 --> 00:28:22.700
The two of you can work together on that.

00:28:23.340 --> 00:28:26.425
Have energy, have excitement.

00:28:26.425 --> 00:28:30.620
Don't let that stress really make you tense.

